    <table cellpadding="2" cellspacing="0" border="0" width="100%" align="center">
    <tr>
        <td bgcolor="#bbbbbb" width="1%" nowrap align="center">
            &nbsp;<font color="#ffffff"><b>Description</b></font>&nbsp;
        </td>
        <td>&nbsp;</td>
    </tr>
    </table>

    <table cellpadding="0" cellspacing="0" border="0" width="100%">
    <tr>
        <td id="descriptionArea">
            <p><b>Title</b><br/>
Successful Refund of Settled Payment</p>

<p><b>Preconditions</b><br/>
1. An order has been successfully created via the Orders API.<br/>
2. The payment has been successfully authorized, captured, and settled.<br/>
3. The order is eligible for a refund according to business rules.<br/>
4. Valid authentication credentials/token are available.<br/>
5. The Refund API endpoint is available and accessible in Postman.<br/>
6. The Payment ID and Order ID are known and available for testing.<br/>
7. The user has permission to process refunds.</p>

<p><b>Test Steps</b><br/>
1. Open Postman.<br/>
2. Retrieve the payment details using the Payment API and verify the payment status is Settled.<br/>
3. Send a POST request to the Refund endpoint.<br/>
4. Include the Payment ID, refund amount, and any required refund reason in the request body.<br/>
5. Include the required authentication token and headers.<br/>
6. Submit the refund request.<br/>
7. Verify that a successful response status code (200 OK or 201 Created) is returned.<br/>
8. Retrieve the refund details using the Refund API.<br/>
9. Retrieve the order details using the Orders API.<br/>
10. Retrieve the transaction history associated with the payment or order.<br/>
11. Attempt to submit the same refund request again to verify duplicate refund prevention.</p>

<p><b>Expected Steps</b><br/>
1. The refund request is processed successfully.<br/>
2. The API returns a successful response status code.<br/>
3. A refund transaction is created and marked as <b>Successful</b>.<br/>
4. The refunded amount matches the original settled payment amount (or the requested refund amount for partial refunds).<br/>
5. The order status is updated appropriately (Refunded/Partially Refunded).<br/>
6. The refund details, refund date, and transaction reference are stored successfully.<br/>
7. The refund transaction appears in the transaction history for audit purposes.<br/>
8. A refund confirmation response is returned by the API.<br/>
9. No duplicate refunds are processed for the same payment request.<br/>
10. If the same refund request is submitted again, the API rejects the request or returns the existing refund record according to business rules.</p>

<p><b>Actual results</b><br/>
Duplicate refunds are processed for the same payment request.</p>
            <br/>
        </td>
    </tr>
    </table>

