    <table cellpadding="2" cellspacing="0" border="0" width="100%" align="center">
    <tr>
        <td bgcolor="#bbbbbb" width="1%" nowrap align="center">
            &nbsp;<font color="#ffffff"><b>Description</b></font>&nbsp;
        </td>
        <td>&nbsp;</td>
    </tr>
    </table>

    <table cellpadding="0" cellspacing="0" border="0" width="100%">
    <tr>
        <td id="descriptionArea">
            <p><b>Title</b> <br/>
Reject Payment with Expired Card</p>

<p><b>Preconditions</b></p>

<ol>
	<li>The payment API endpoint is available and accessible through Postman.</li>
	<li>A valid API authentication token or credentials are available (if required).</li>
	<li>A payment request payload structure is available.</li>
	<li>An expired test card is available for testing.</li>
	<li>The payment service is operational.</li>
</ol>


<p><b>Test steps</b><br/>
1. Open Postman.<br/>
2. Create a <b>POST</b> request to the payment API endpoint.<br/>
3. Add the required headers (e.g., <tt>Content-Type: application/json</tt> and authorization token if applicable).<br/>
4. In the request body, enter valid payment details, but use an expired card expiry date<br/>
5. Send the request.<br/>
6. Review the API response, status code, and response body.</p>

<p><b>Expected Results</b> <br/>
1. The payment request is rejected.<br/>
2. The API returns the appropriate error status code <tt>400 Bad Request</tt> <br/>
3. The response body contains an error message indicating that the card has expired.<br/>
4. No payment authorization or capture is performed.<br/>
5. No order or payment transaction is created.<br/>
6. The error response follows the API's standard error format.<br/>
7. Sensitive card information is not exposed in the response.</p>

<p><b>Actual Results</b><br/>
The payment request is accepted. <br/>
The API returns status code 201 Created </p>
            <br/>
        </td>
    </tr>
    </table>

